'''''Mammillaria spinosissima''''' ({{IPAc-en|ˌ|m|æ|m|ɪ|ˈ|l|ɛər|i|ə|_|ˌ|s|p|ɪ|n|oʊ|ˈ|s|ɪ|s|ɪ|m|ə}}), also known as the '''spiny pincushion cactus''', is a [[species]] of [[flowering plant]] in the cactus [[family (botany)|family]] Cactaceae, [[endemic]] to the central Mexican states of [[Guerrero]] and [[Morelos]], where they grow at elevations of approximately {{convert|1600|to|1900|m}}. The species was [[Species description|described]] in 1838 by [[James Forbes (botanist)|James Forbes]], gardener of the [[Duke of Bedford]]. Botanist [[David Hunt (botanist)|David Hunt]] collected a specimen in 1971, when he located one near [[Sierra de Tepoztlan]], Mexico.

''M. spinosissima'' thrive in well-drained soils that are sandy or [[loam]], with a [[pH]] ranging from acidic to neutral. They prefer low humidity and full, filtered sun. Plants are typically watered once every two or three weeks, and kept nearly dry during the winter months. They require no pruning and make good patio and container plants. They are relatively disease resistant, but susceptible to pests such as [[mealybug]]s. Synonyms of ''M. spinosissima'' include ''Mammillaria centraliplumosa'', ''Mammillaria haasii'', and ''Mammillaria virginis''.

==Taxonomy==
[[File:Mexico.Mor.Tepoztlan.01.jpg|left|thumb|alt=A color picture of a mountain from town|Sierra de Tepoztlan]]
[[Carl Linnaeus]] designated ''[[Mammillaria]]'' as a type [[genus]] for [[cactus]] in 1753.{{sfn|Butterworth|Wallace|2004|p=1086}} In 1838, [[James Forbes (botanist)|James Forbes]], gardener of the [[Duke of Bedford]], listed and [[Species description|described]] a species he called ''Echinocactus spinosissimas'' from a group of cacti he had acquired in Europe three years earlier. [[Nathaniel Lord Britton]] and [[Joseph Nelson Rose]] believe that Forbes was given that name by [[Ludwig Karl Georg Pfeiffer]], but the plant was actually ''Mammillaria spinosissima''.{{sfn|Britton|Rose|1922|p=179}}

Pfeiffer had published the first infrageneric division of ''Mammillaria'' in 1837, dividing the genus into two groups based on distinct spine characteristics. In 1845, [[Joseph zu Salm-Reifferscheidt-Dyck]]&nbsp;– based on work by [[Frederick Scheer]]&nbsp;– expanded the classification into eight groups. With at least 145 recognized species, it is one of the largest and most morphologically variable genera in the cactus family.{{sfn|Butterworth|Wallace|2004|p=1086}} Others estimate there are as many as two hundred species of ''Mammillaria'', including sixty-two cultivated species from India. Though larger, the genus ''[[Opuntia]]'' is less popular with gardeners and landscapers.{{sfn|Mattagajasingh|Mukherjee|Das|2006|p=583}} ''Mammillaria'' was previously thought to be [[Monophyly|monophyletic]], but [[Phylogenetics|phylogenic]] analysis indicates that ''Mammilloydia'' is "embedded within a 'core' group of ''Mammillaria'' species."{{sfn|Butterworth|Wallace|2004|p=1086}} A specimen of ''Mammillaria spinosissima'' was collected by Botanist [[David Hunt (botanist)|David Hunt]] in September 1971, when he located one in Mexico, near the Morelos Cautla-Cuernavaca toll road in [[Sierra de Tepoztlan]], at an altitude of {{convert|1600|meter}}.{{sfn|Kew Gardens: Herbarium catalogue}}

===Similar species, subspecies and synonyms===
Species similar to ''Mammillaria spinosissima'' include ''[[Mammillaria backebergiana]]'' and ''[[Mammillaria meyranii]]''.{{sfn|Graham|2014|p=105}} Subspecies include ''M. spinosissima pilcayensis'', synonym: ''Bravo'' (D. R. Hunt), ''M. spinosissima tepoxtlana'' (D. R. Hunt), and ''M. spinosissima spinosissima'' (Lem.).{{sfn|Integrated Taxonomic Information System (a)}} Synonyms of ''M. spinosissima'' include ''Mammillaria centraliplumosa'' (Fittkau), ''Mammillaria haasii'' (J. Meyrán), and ''Mammillaria virginis'' (Fittkau and Kladiwa).{{sfn|Integrated Taxonomic Information System (b)}}

==Description==
[[File:Mammillaria spinosissima3.jpg|right|thumb|alt=A color picture of a reddish-spined cactus|In the [[United States Botanic Garden]]]]
''Mammillaria spinosissima'', also known as spiny pincushion cactus, are cylindrical plants that grow up to {{convert|30|cm}} tall and {{convert|10|cm}} wide. They reach full height after five to ten years. The spines are red-brown or white, with cream-colored radials and pink, funnel-shaped flowers that grow in a ring around the apex of the stem to approximately {{convert|2|cm}} long.{{sfn|Royal Horticultural Society}} The relatively small cacti are globular or elongated, and the flowers produce generally bright red berries that are club-shaped, smooth, and juicy.{{sfn|Mattagajasingh|Mukherjee|Das|2006|p=583}}

''Mammillaria'' species tend to grow low to the ground in solitary or grouped in clusters. The genus is marked by its dimorphic [[areole]]s: the spine bearing, or vegetative, areoles are located on the apex of the tubercle, and flowering areoles are located inside the axils of the tubercles.{{sfn|Butterworth|Wallace|2004|p=1086}} The stem is woolly and covered with bristles.{{sfn|Pienaar|2000|p=221}}

==Native habitat==
The genus ''Mammillaria''{{'s}} native habitat ranges from Colombia and Venezuela to the Southwestern United States. Its diversity is greatest in Mexico.{{sfn|Butterworth|Wallace|2004|p=1086}} Species have also been documented in the West Indies.{{sfn|Mattagajasingh|Mukherjee|Das|2006|p=583}} ''M. spinosissima'' is [[endemic]] to central Mexico and concentrated in the states of [[Guerrero]] and [[Morelos]], where they grow at elevations of approximately {{convert|1600 to 1900|meter}}; they prefer dry, tropical forests and xerophilous scrub.{{sfn|International Union for Conservation of Nature and Natural Resources}} The subspecies ''pilcayensis'' is named for its occurrence in the Barranca de Pilcaya, in Guerrero.{{sfn|Eggli|Newton|2004|p=185}}

==Cultivation==
Britton and Rose believe that ''M. spinosissima'' has been in cultivation since at least 1835.{{sfn|Britton|Rose|1922|p=179}} The species thrives in well-drained soils that are sandy or [[loam]], with a [[pH]] that is acidic, alkaline, or neutral. They prefer low humidity, and grow well under glass, with full, filtered sun from the south, north, and east. Plants are typically watered once every couple of weeks, and kept nearly dry during the winter months. Propagation is by offsets or by seeds sown in early spring in {{convert|19 to 24|C-change|0}} weather. They require no pruning and make good patio and container plants. They are disease resistant, but are susceptible to pests such as [[mealybugs]].{{sfn|Royal Horticultural Society}} This plant has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref name = RHSPF>{{cite web | url= https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/10843/i-Mammillaria-spinosissima-i/Details | title = RHS Plantfinder - ''Mammillaria spinosissima'' | access-date=3 April 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web  | url= https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/pdfs/agm-lists/agm-ornamentals.pdf | title = AGM Plants - Ornamental | date = July 2017 | page = 63 
| publisher = Royal Horticultural Society | access-date = 25 March 2018}}</ref>

